@flexiarg or3/count-every-card-back
@title Count Every Card Back
@audience workshop facilitators, programme leads, anyone running elicitation
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🎴/源 🧵/迹]
@see or3/co-design-the-call, or/attribution-forward-review

! conclusion: Count Every Card Back

  + context: You have run a workshop in which people contributed ideas, and you now have to do something with them.

  + IF:
    A group has generated raw material — cards, post-its, topic descriptions — that will be themed, merged, and narrowed before it becomes anything public.

  + HOWEVER:
    Synthesis is where attribution dies. Ideas get clustered, reworded, and combined, and by the time a clean list exists nobody can say whose contribution became what. Contributors who cannot see themselves in the output stop contributing, and "we co-designed this" quietly becomes untrue.

  + THEN:
    Keep a trace from every item in the final set back to its origin, and say at the outset that you are doing so. Treat this as a distinct obligation from publishing the material: the process can be fully open — visible criteria, visible voting, visible lineage — while the ideas themselves stay within the contributor pool. Open is not the same as disseminated, and saying so protects contributors who would otherwise self-censor.

  + BECAUSE:
    Traceability is what makes shared ownership checkable rather than asserted. It also resolves the apparent conflict between openness and idea protection: people will put their good ideas into a process that can prove where they went, and will withhold them from one that cannot.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Decide and announce, before the workshop, who the elicited material may be shared with.]
    next[Keep the raw-to-themed mapping as a working artefact; it is the evidence that the process was open.]
